/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Direct coupling puts the fiber face close to the source with no lens.
   - Radiance L is power per area per solid angle, in W m⁻² sr⁻¹.
   - η is coupling efficiency (0 to 1); Pf is power in the guided mode (W); Ps is power emitted by the source (W).
   - NA = sin θa is the fiber numerical aperture; θa is the acceptance angle in air; the source area is no larger than the core and fills a hemisphere.
   - For a Lambertian source the fraction of emitted power inside the acceptance cone is sin²θa = NA², so NA enters squared.
   - In terms of the source radiance B0 (W cm⁻² sr⁻¹) and emitting radius rs, a Lambertian surface emits Ps = π²rs²B0 in all.
   - Fresnel reflection at an uncoated air-glass face (n ≈ 1.5) removes near 4% of the light, R = ((n − 1)/(n + 1))² ≈ 0.04, even with perfect alignment.
   - When the source area is larger than the core, only the part of the source that images onto the core can couple.
   - rc is core radius (m); rs is source emitting radius (m); NA is numerical aperture.
   - A lens can match the source image to the core, but shrinking the image widens the ray angles at the fiber face, so for a large Lambertian source a lens cannot raise η above the (rc/rs)² NA² limit.
*/
